{
  "dom_id": "step-q96828",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14241806/assets/1b124a0e4439f1b35affa5a38f175a151cbbad88affb12738585da8280bacb23.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The domain of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$\n\nIII. The range of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\}$",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X=\\{0,1\\},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y=\\{0,1,2,3\\},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is $\\{0,1\\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
